1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:02,937
A life sentence is one thing.

2
00:00:02,957 --> 00:00:05,973
Serving it above the Arctic Circle is another.

3
00:00:07,293 --> 00:00:09,885
In a small Russian village whose name,

4
00:00:09,905 --> 00:00:11,752
in the local Nenets language,

5
00:00:11,772 --> 00:00:14,737
means northern lights, there are two prison colonies,

6
00:00:14,757 --> 00:00:17,350
just down the road from each other.

7
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:20,215
In one of them,

8
00:00:20,235 --> 00:00:23,346
Alexei Navalny died in February twenty twenty four.

9
00:00:23,366 --> 00:00:26,867
The other holds some of Russia's most notorious killers,

10
00:00:26,887 --> 00:00:29,606
and they are never supposed to leave.

11
00:00:29,626 --> 00:00:31,563
Its name is Polar Owl.

12
00:00:32,883 --> 00:00:36,568
This year, according to the investigative outlet The Insider,

13
00:00:36,588 --> 00:00:39,861
a prosecutor found injured prisoners inside Polar Owl,

14
00:00:39,881 --> 00:00:42,331
and cameras that had not recorded.

15
00:00:42,351 --> 00:00:46,035
At Polar Owl, the weather is only the beginning.

16
00:00:48,575 --> 00:00:50,896
Chapter 1. The end of the line.

17
00:00:55,496 --> 00:00:57,239
The village is called Kharp.

18
00:00:57,259 --> 00:01:00,412
It sits in the Yamal Nenets region of northern

19
00:01:00,432 --> 00:01:03,233
Russia, at the foot of the Polar Urals,

20
00:01:03,253 --> 00:01:06,407
on the eastern side of the mountains that divide

21
00:01:06,427 --> 00:01:07,464
Europe from Asia.

22
00:01:08,784 --> 00:01:10,536
The nearest city, Salekhard,

23
00:01:10,556 --> 00:01:13,637
lies almost exactly on the Arctic Circle.

24
00:01:13,657 --> 00:01:16,738
Kharp is to the north of it.

25
00:01:16,758 --> 00:01:20,725
From Moscow, a train runs to the town of

26
00:01:20,745 --> 00:01:22,941
Labytnangi, about thirty kilometers away.

27
00:01:22,961 --> 00:01:25,599
After that, there is a road.

28
00:01:25,619 --> 00:01:29,586
And then, there is nothing but tundra and forest.

29
00:01:30,906 --> 00:01:34,597
In Salekhard, the average January night falls to around

30
00:01:34,617 --> 00:01:36,659
minus twenty eight degrees Celsius.

31
00:01:36,679 --> 00:01:39,957
The record is close to minus fifty two.

32
00:01:39,977 --> 00:01:42,431
For seven months of the year,

33
00:01:42,451 --> 00:01:44,906
the average temperature stays below zero.

34
00:01:44,926 --> 00:01:48,617
And in December, the weather records show almost no

35
00:01:48,637 --> 00:01:49,854
sunshine at all.

36
00:01:51,174 --> 00:01:53,885
About five thousand people live in Kharp.

37
00:01:53,905 --> 00:01:56,226
For the men inside Polar Owl,

38
00:01:56,246 --> 00:01:59,348
this is not a stop on a journey.

39
00:01:59,368 --> 00:02:00,909
It is the destination.

40
00:02:03,249 --> 00:02:06,266
Chapter 2. Built by prisoners.

41
00:02:10,866 --> 00:02:14,601
The story of Kharp begins with a railway.

42
00:02:14,621 --> 00:02:18,826
Under Stalin, Gulag prisoners were sent to build a

43
00:02:18,846 --> 00:02:20,704
line across the Arctic,

44
00:02:20,724 --> 00:02:22,582
from Salekhard towards Igarka,

45
00:02:22,602 --> 00:02:24,460
through swamp and permafrost.

46
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:28,596
After Stalin's death in nineteen fifty three,

47
00:02:28,616 --> 00:02:31,028
much of that project was abandoned.

48
00:02:31,048 --> 00:02:34,675
Sections of rusting track were left in the tundra.

49
00:02:34,695 --> 00:02:37,917
Russians came to call it the Dead Road.

50
00:02:40,037 --> 00:02:42,615
But the camps did not disappear.

51
00:02:42,635 --> 00:02:44,347
According to Russian sources,

52
00:02:44,367 --> 00:02:48,244
the neighboring colony was founded in nineteen sixty one,

53
00:02:48,264 --> 00:02:52,141
on the site of a former camp unit of

54
00:02:52,161 --> 00:02:53,873
the Gulag railway construction.

55
00:02:53,893 --> 00:02:57,769
Six years later, the first prisoners described as especially

56
00:02:57,789 --> 00:02:59,501
dangerous repeat offenders arrived.

57
00:03:00,821 --> 00:03:04,204
Polar Owl grew out of the same history.

58
00:03:04,224 --> 00:03:08,032
It became a prison for particularly dangerous repeat offenders.

59
00:03:08,052 --> 00:03:10,159
And in twenty oh five,

60
00:03:10,179 --> 00:03:12,285
according to Russian reference sources,

61
00:03:12,305 --> 00:03:14,412
it became something more permanent.

62
00:03:14,432 --> 00:03:17,389
A colony for men sentenced to life.

63
00:03:19,729 --> 00:03:22,004
Chapter 3. Life.

64
00:03:26,604 --> 00:03:31,118
Russia has not carried out an execution since nineteen

65
00:03:31,138 --> 00:03:35,653
ninety six. Instead, its most serious criminals receive life

66
00:03:35,673 --> 00:03:36,157
imprisonment.

67
00:03:37,477 --> 00:03:40,350
By law, a life sentence cannot be given to

68
00:03:40,370 --> 00:03:43,243
women, to anyone who was under eighteen at the

69
00:03:43,263 --> 00:03:44,529
time of the crime,

70
00:03:44,549 --> 00:03:47,422
or to men aged sixty five or older at

71
00:03:47,442 --> 00:03:47,744
sentencing.

72
00:03:49,064 --> 00:03:52,514
Russia holds its lifers in a small number of

73
00:03:52,534 --> 00:03:55,599
special colonies. Polar Owl is one of seven.

74
00:03:55,619 --> 00:03:57,527
According to Russian reference sources,

75
00:03:57,547 --> 00:04:00,998
there were one thousand nine hundred and forty two

76
00:04:01,018 --> 00:04:04,468
people serving life in Russia at the end of

77
00:04:04,488 --> 00:04:05,625
twenty twenty two.

78
00:04:06,945 --> 00:04:10,727
Polar Owl is officially listed with one thousand and

79
00:04:10,747 --> 00:04:14,529
fourteen places. Another Russian source puts its limit for

80
00:04:14,549 --> 00:04:17,487
lifers at four hundred and sixty four.

81
00:04:17,507 --> 00:04:21,289
The current number of prisoners has not been published.

82
00:04:23,429 --> 00:04:26,283
Chapter 4. Who is inside.

83
00:04:30,883 --> 00:04:34,170
Russian media and reference sources have named several notorious

84
00:04:34,190 --> 00:04:36,007
prisoners held at Polar Owl.

85
00:04:36,027 --> 00:04:39,313
We have not been able to confirm each one

86
00:04:39,333 --> 00:04:40,783
with the prison service,

87
00:04:40,803 --> 00:04:42,620
so treat these as reports.

88
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:46,523
Among them, according to these reports,

89
00:04:46,543 --> 00:04:50,429
is Nurpashi Kulayev, the only hostage taker captured alive

90
00:04:50,449 --> 00:04:54,335
after the Beslan school siege in twenty oh four,

91
00:04:54,355 --> 00:04:58,240
in which more than three hundred people were killed,

92
00:04:58,260 --> 00:04:59,976
many of them children.

93
00:05:01,296 --> 00:05:04,524
Also reported to be there is Alexander Pichushkin,

94
00:05:04,544 --> 00:05:07,366
the man known as the Chessboard Killer,

95
00:05:07,386 --> 00:05:11,020
convicted in twenty oh seven of dozens of murders

96
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:12,644
in a Moscow park.

97
00:05:12,664 --> 00:05:16,298
And Denis Yevsyukov, a former police major who opened

98
00:05:16,318 --> 00:05:19,952
fire in a Moscow supermarket in twenty oh nine.

99
00:05:21,272 --> 00:05:24,094
These are men whose names Russia remembers.

100
00:05:24,114 --> 00:05:27,342
Inside Polar Owl, what matters is something else.

101
00:05:27,362 --> 00:05:30,996
Each of them is serving a sentence that has

102
00:05:31,016 --> 00:05:32,214
no end date.

103
00:05:34,534 --> 00:05:38,302
If you want more stories from inside the world's

104
00:05:38,322 --> 00:05:42,091
hardest prisons, subscribe. We release new ones every week.

105
00:05:44,231 --> 00:05:46,516
Chapter 5. The cell.

106
00:05:51,116 --> 00:05:54,713
Every lifer in Russia begins in the same place.

107
00:05:54,733 --> 00:05:58,329
By law, he arrives under what are called strict

108
00:05:58,349 --> 00:05:58,731
conditions.

109
00:06:00,051 --> 00:06:01,455
He lives in a cell,

110
00:06:01,475 --> 00:06:04,019
as a rule with no more than one other

111
00:06:04,039 --> 00:06:05,728
man. He can be held alone,

112
00:06:05,748 --> 00:06:06,867
at his own request,

113
00:06:06,887 --> 00:06:09,430
or if the colony decides he is a threat.

114
00:06:10,750 --> 00:06:14,038
Each day, he is allowed one and a half

115
00:06:14,058 --> 00:06:16,977
hours of walking, in a small enclosed yard.

116
00:06:16,997 --> 00:06:19,549
According to accounts from Russian lifer colonies,

117
00:06:19,569 --> 00:06:22,489
prisoners are often handcuffed whenever they are moved,

118
00:06:22,509 --> 00:06:25,796
and are not allowed to lie on their beds

119
00:06:25,816 --> 00:06:26,899
during the day.

120
00:06:28,219 --> 00:06:30,770
The law sets out the rest.

121
00:06:30,790 --> 00:06:34,627
Under strict conditions, a lifer may have two short

122
00:06:34,647 --> 00:06:38,055
visits a year, of up to four hours,

123
00:06:38,075 --> 00:06:41,055
with staff present. And one long visit.

124
00:06:41,075 --> 00:06:42,769
One parcel a year.

125
00:06:42,789 --> 00:06:45,769
Phone calls, only in exceptional personal circumstances.

126
00:06:47,089 --> 00:06:49,267
For a family living in Moscow,

127
00:06:49,287 --> 00:06:52,565
the trip to Kharp means two thousand kilometers or

128
00:06:52,585 --> 00:06:54,764
more, much of it by train.

129
00:06:54,784 --> 00:06:58,061
For many prisoners, those two short visits a year

130
00:06:58,081 --> 00:06:59,893
may never happen at all.

131
00:07:02,233 --> 00:07:05,007
Chapter 6. Ten years.

132
00:07:09,607 --> 00:07:13,070
There is one way to change these conditions.

133
00:07:13,090 --> 00:07:14,377
Time, and obedience.

134
00:07:15,697 --> 00:07:19,609
If a lifer completes ten years under strict conditions

135
00:07:19,629 --> 00:07:23,541
without a violation, he can be moved to ordinary

136
00:07:23,561 --> 00:07:25,725
conditions. Two hours of walking.

137
00:07:25,745 --> 00:07:29,657
Two short visits and two long visits a year.

138
00:07:29,677 --> 00:07:31,842
After another ten clean years,

139
00:07:31,862 --> 00:07:35,337
easier conditions. Any violation can send him back.

140
00:07:36,657 --> 00:07:38,403
After twenty five years,

141
00:07:38,423 --> 00:07:41,051
a lifer can apply for parole.

142
00:07:41,071 --> 00:07:43,258
According to Russian reference sources,

143
00:07:43,278 --> 00:07:46,789
no lifer has yet been released that way.

144
00:07:46,809 --> 00:07:50,762
In twenty thirteen, a court did grant parole to

145
00:07:50,782 --> 00:07:52,969
one prisoner in another colony.

146
00:07:52,989 --> 00:07:54,735
The decision was overturned.

147
00:07:56,055 --> 00:07:59,931
A few men have left Polar Owl by another

148
00:07:59,951 --> 00:08:02,096
route. According to Russian media,

149
00:08:02,116 --> 00:08:04,261
a prisoner named Anvar Masalimov,

150
00:08:04,281 --> 00:08:05,993
once sentenced to death,

151
00:08:06,013 --> 00:08:09,457
was freed after a court reviewed his sentence.

152
00:08:09,477 --> 00:08:11,622
But for almost everyone inside,

153
00:08:11,642 --> 00:08:14,220
there is no plan for release.

154
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:16,385
Only the next ten years.

155
00:08:18,925 --> 00:08:21,709
Chapter 7. Confessions.

156
00:08:26,309 --> 00:08:29,236
Polar Owl has been at the center of scandal

157
00:08:29,256 --> 00:08:29,563
before.

158
00:08:30,883 --> 00:08:34,873
In twenty thirteen, the newspaper Novaya Gazeta reported that

159
00:08:34,893 --> 00:08:38,882
prisoners at the colony had written around one hundred

160
00:08:38,902 --> 00:08:42,891
and ninety confessions to crimes they had not committed.

161
00:08:42,911 --> 00:08:46,900
Another newspaper, Izvestia, later reported a figure of thirty

162
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:47,345
two.

163
00:08:48,665 --> 00:08:50,575
According to these reports,

164
00:08:50,595 --> 00:08:54,435
guards were suspected of beating prisoners into confessing.

165
00:08:54,455 --> 00:08:58,777
Novaya Gazeta reported that the regional prison service tried

166
00:08:58,797 --> 00:09:01,190
to keep the story quiet.

167
00:09:01,210 --> 00:09:02,637
These remain allegations.

168
00:09:03,957 --> 00:09:06,719
For a man already serving life,

169
00:09:06,739 --> 00:09:09,038
a new conviction changes little.

170
00:09:09,058 --> 00:09:10,892
For a police statistic,

171
00:09:10,912 --> 00:09:13,211
an old unsolved case closes.

172
00:09:13,231 --> 00:09:15,529
That is why, critics said,

173
00:09:15,549 --> 00:09:17,384
the scheme could work.

174
00:09:19,724 --> 00:09:22,102
Chapter 8. The neighbor.

175
00:09:26,702 --> 00:09:30,123
Just down the road stands the other colony,

176
00:09:30,143 --> 00:09:33,133
IK three. Prisoners call it Polar Wolf.

177
00:09:34,453 --> 00:09:37,205
It is not a colony for lifers,

178
00:09:37,225 --> 00:09:40,768
but a special regime colony for men serving long

179
00:09:40,788 --> 00:09:44,332
terms. Prisoners who passed through it described to the

180
00:09:44,352 --> 00:09:46,312
New York Times unbearable cold,

181
00:09:46,332 --> 00:09:47,896
terrible food, and beatings.

182
00:09:49,216 --> 00:09:51,672
In December twenty twenty three,

183
00:09:51,692 --> 00:09:55,138
Alexei Navalny, Russia's best known opposition politician,

184
00:09:55,158 --> 00:09:58,108
serving nineteen years, was transferred there.

185
00:09:58,128 --> 00:10:01,079
On February sixteenth, twenty twenty four,

186
00:10:01,099 --> 00:10:05,040
the prison service announced that he had died,

187
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:08,011
after losing consciousness following a walk.

188
00:10:08,031 --> 00:10:09,992
He was forty seven.

189
00:10:11,312 --> 00:10:15,473
Russia's Investigative Committee later concluded that his death was

190
00:10:15,493 --> 00:10:18,261
caused by a combination of illnesses,

191
00:10:18,281 --> 00:10:21,049
and that there was no crime.

192
00:10:21,069 --> 00:10:22,908
His wife, Yulia Navalnaya,

193
00:10:22,928 --> 00:10:24,302
rejected that conclusion.

194
00:10:25,622 --> 00:10:28,092
In February twenty twenty six,

195
00:10:28,112 --> 00:10:32,575
five European governments said that tests on samples had

196
00:10:32,595 --> 00:10:35,066
found a toxin called epibatidine.

197
00:10:35,086 --> 00:10:38,055
Russia called the accusations absolutely unfounded.

198
00:10:39,375 --> 00:10:43,707
The United States, Britain and the European Union sanctioned

199
00:10:43,727 --> 00:10:46,609
the colony's leadership. In its decision,

200
00:10:46,629 --> 00:10:50,961
the European Union described the colony as known for

201
00:10:50,981 --> 00:10:52,896
physical and psychological pressure,

202
00:10:52,916 --> 00:10:55,314
total isolation, torture and violence.

203
00:10:57,854 --> 00:11:00,522
Chapter 9. Lubyanka.

204
00:11:05,122 --> 00:11:07,219
In April twenty twenty six,

205
00:11:07,239 --> 00:11:10,182
a new prisoner arrived at Polar Owl.

206
00:11:10,202 --> 00:11:12,299
He was not a lifer.

207
00:11:12,319 --> 00:11:15,262
Azat Miftakhov is a mathematician and anarchist,

208
00:11:15,282 --> 00:11:19,072
who had spent most of the previous year in

209
00:11:19,092 --> 00:11:19,919
solitary confinement.

210
00:11:21,239 --> 00:11:22,931
According to his account,

211
00:11:22,951 --> 00:11:24,643
published by The Insider,

212
00:11:24,663 --> 00:11:28,067
he was taken to the colony's operational department,

213
00:11:28,087 --> 00:11:30,207
a place prisoners call Lubyanka,

214
00:11:30,227 --> 00:11:34,060
after the old headquarters of the Soviet secret police.

215
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:39,061
Miftakhov alleges that, after he refused to clean a

216
00:11:39,081 --> 00:11:41,118
toilet, he was taped up,

217
00:11:41,138 --> 00:11:42,763
beaten on the heels,

218
00:11:42,783 --> 00:11:45,642
given electric shocks while loud music played,

219
00:11:45,662 --> 00:11:47,287
and threatened with rape.

220
00:11:47,307 --> 00:11:49,344
In an interview, he said,

221
00:11:49,364 --> 00:11:51,400
I started screaming in pain.

222
00:11:52,720 --> 00:11:55,215
The colony's side is different.

223
00:11:55,235 --> 00:11:59,741
The regional prosecutor told his lawyers that an inspection

224
00:11:59,761 --> 00:12:02,759
had found no evidence of violence.

225
00:12:02,779 --> 00:12:07,286
A medical examination, fifteen days after the alleged torture,

226
00:12:07,306 --> 00:12:09,801
found no injuries. In June,

227
00:12:09,821 --> 00:12:14,327
the Investigative Committee refused to open a criminal case,

228
00:12:14,347 --> 00:12:15,836
citing insufficient evidence.

229
00:12:17,156 --> 00:12:20,687
These are allegations, and they have not been proven

230
00:12:20,707 --> 00:12:24,237
in court. But Miftakhov's complaints led to something else.

231
00:12:26,777 --> 00:12:29,167
Chapter 10. The cameras.

232
00:12:33,767 --> 00:12:36,158
In June twenty twenty six,

233
00:12:36,178 --> 00:12:39,533
after the complaints, prosecutors inspected Polar Owl.

234
00:12:39,553 --> 00:12:41,462
According to The Insider,

235
00:12:41,482 --> 00:12:43,873
which reviewed the prosecutor's filing,

236
00:12:43,893 --> 00:12:46,283
they found two new prisoners,

237
00:12:46,303 --> 00:12:50,623
both lifers, who had arrived days earlier with physical

238
00:12:50,643 --> 00:12:53,034
injuries. The injuries were photographed.

239
00:12:54,354 --> 00:12:58,019
One of them said staff had beaten him on

240
00:12:58,039 --> 00:12:59,657
the day he arrived.

241
00:12:59,677 --> 00:13:03,343
The colony's logs contained no mention of the injuries.

242
00:13:03,363 --> 00:13:06,619
Entries were added only after the prosecutor's intervention.

243
00:13:07,939 --> 00:13:10,995
And the cameras. According to the filing,

244
00:13:11,015 --> 00:13:14,950
no body cameras were used during the searches on

245
00:13:14,970 --> 00:13:17,147
the day the prisoners arrived.

246
00:13:17,167 --> 00:13:19,784
The fixed cameras failed to record.

247
00:13:19,804 --> 00:13:21,542
At the medical examinations,

248
00:13:21,562 --> 00:13:23,739
no portable recorders were used.

249
00:13:23,759 --> 00:13:27,254
The prosecutor found that this violated the rules.

250
00:13:28,574 --> 00:13:32,207
The filing does not conclude that staff caused the

251
00:13:32,227 --> 00:13:35,860
injuries. The Insider says it has no information on

252
00:13:35,880 --> 00:13:37,484
whether anyone was disciplined.

253
00:13:37,504 --> 00:13:41,136
A former analyst of the Russian prison service told

254
00:13:41,156 --> 00:13:44,789
The Insider that missing recordings like these protect the

255
00:13:44,809 --> 00:13:46,413
institution from later claims.

256
00:13:48,533 --> 00:13:52,459
Miftakhov's lawyer, meanwhile, says colony staff seized his written

257
00:13:52,479 --> 00:13:56,406
notes, and the colony issued her a formal warning

258
00:13:56,426 --> 00:14:00,353
for antisocial behavior. She has taken the colony to

259
00:14:00,373 --> 00:14:00,791
court.

260
00:14:03,131 --> 00:14:05,800
Chapter 11. For life.

261
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:13,834
This is what life looks like at Polar Owl.

262
00:14:13,854 --> 00:14:16,521
A cell, shared with one other man.

263
00:14:16,541 --> 00:14:19,975
An hour and a half in a small yard.

264
00:14:19,995 --> 00:14:21,894
Two short visits a year,

265
00:14:21,914 --> 00:14:24,197
if anyone can make the journey.

266
00:14:24,217 --> 00:14:26,499
And outside, seven months of winter.

267
00:14:27,819 --> 00:14:31,173
The men inside include some of the worst criminals

268
00:14:31,193 --> 00:14:32,672
in Russia's modern history.

269
